[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186198272 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-871c3eee-78f1-5d74-b0c7-258b8f08a0ed" "question-68fa150f-fc38-56f5-947b-bb3d8c8fe527"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287870 :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=\\{-2,2\\}\\times \\{-1,0,1\\}$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing $\\{-2,2\\}\\times \\{-1,0,1\\}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS=\\{(-2,-1),(-2,0),(-2,1),(2,-1),(2,0),(2,1)\\}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/5d/5dd3ff71d17188adaa68632b6d16aed1f41e1975ec4ddd321a8160fabb0e16b8.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-871c3eee-78f1-5d74-b0c7-258b8f08a0ed" :question/id #uuid "871c3eee-78f1-5d74-b0c7-258b8f08a0ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-182004" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=\\{-2,1,2\\}\\times [0,2]$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=\\{-2,1,2\\}\\times [0,2]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $-2,1,$ or $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,2].$\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in \\{-2,1,2\\},\\,y\\in [0,2]\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,2].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,2]\\}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/44/449b948df4a32c540281ddac1659c261e2908433ecc1f383b26cb5cb4aa29756.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,2].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}=\\{(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,2]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/92/922c96e62e8ed3481e595595e060956fbaf191cd3ec7636e7a874b11fea3bcd5.png)\n\nNow, consider ${S}_{3}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,2].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}=\\{(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,2]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/0d/0d3cb62f7e9fb47823d0a2ba8c071e09adbea673ad54a5d1b014e8882aeff472.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the three line segments shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7cecda03e84263537cb29bbd14bb4dccc2e60fada10db1ca96798490e978676.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-120de378-6c5c-5a20-a5a4-fe230e702451"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-120de378-6c5c-5a20-a5a4-fe230e702451" :answer-field/id #uuid "120de378-6c5c-5a20-a5a4-fe230e702451" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8ef1cf93-ea8f-5c4a-bd63-007805fb0442" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c442a1fb-c5aa-593e-8502-90bebc8b66ae" "answer-1cb0b49a-e727-5e96-8f08-b6642b88f5fb" "answer-3f98f313-1f05-5432-9f13-6963c25952a4" "answer-d7da440a-e81b-550c-ac55-bea9848fa4dc" "answer-8ef1cf93-ea8f-5c4a-bd63-007805fb0442"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c442a1fb-c5aa-593e-8502-90bebc8b66ae" :answer/id #uuid "c442a1fb-c5aa-593e-8502-90bebc8b66ae" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/13/13a0024b41d50d0ad22f790246e17ec19ce1e9bf91c7e2fe734bfcbdb9a00ef9.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1cb0b49a-e727-5e96-8f08-b6642b88f5fb" :answer/id #uuid "1cb0b49a-e727-5e96-8f08-b6642b88f5fb" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/21/21119e9bd9b5a183cbf686891ba1b1fb33f645fbff27f1e088af5a88e5eea026.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f98f313-1f05-5432-9f13-6963c25952a4" :answer/id #uuid "3f98f313-1f05-5432-9f13-6963c25952a4" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/a4/a4b3570ea09a38f044c6926f0516ddf9c42f9af6953422e3f2b90bca07bf70e9.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7da440a-e81b-550c-ac55-bea9848fa4dc" :answer/id #uuid "d7da440a-e81b-550c-ac55-bea9848fa4dc" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b8/b871bd5b8829c981f43bfbf8c051d50ee7a389df6c706f5d48fd07e167d9a66e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ef1cf93-ea8f-5c4a-bd63-007805fb0442" :answer/id #uuid "8ef1cf93-ea8f-5c4a-bd63-007805fb0442" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/f7/f7cecda03e84263537cb29bbd14bb4dccc2e60fada10db1ca96798490e978676.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198802 :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=\\{0,1,2\\}\\times \\{-1,1\\}$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing $\\{0,1,2\\}\\times \\{-1,1\\}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS=\\{(0,-1),(0,1),(1,-1),(1,1),(2,-1),(2,1)\\}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/c5/c5fdae9b803df3873ee7deef4a36678e0d8274dd256fe331360261f1fe6dd492.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-68fa150f-fc38-56f5-947b-bb3d8c8fe527" :question/id #uuid "68fa150f-fc38-56f5-947b-bb3d8c8fe527" :question/math-academy-id "q-184490" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=\\{-2,0,2\\}\\times \\{1,4\\}$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing $\\{-2,0,2\\}\\times \\{1,4\\}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS=\\{(-2,1),(-2,4),(0,1),(0,4),(2,1),(2,4)\\}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/86/86598866633d3a49c13cf979b0c2c9de74d147e4584a1c6a88847434bc9d3bbf.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9f0af83d-008f-57e3-8335-76248a77c51e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9f0af83d-008f-57e3-8335-76248a77c51e" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f0af83d-008f-57e3-8335-76248a77c51e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a5c8c8e1-f272-595a-96c9-7d04213ad8aa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-06dab824-32e7-5fbe-a9fd-1e57e69a4a62" "answer-0b53377f-4bd5-5365-9c10-2ca3b11194d0" "answer-541ae966-8a6c-5dc2-a2a7-73778aa00ec1" "answer-a5c8c8e1-f272-595a-96c9-7d04213ad8aa" "answer-7335149e-fb88-517d-9f8a-6c7ddaf63411"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06dab824-32e7-5fbe-a9fd-1e57e69a4a62" :answer/id #uuid "06dab824-32e7-5fbe-a9fd-1e57e69a4a62" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/05/05721c578d17e26442cb64b114e64d52fb3822e2cd33a852144be05669087737.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b53377f-4bd5-5365-9c10-2ca3b11194d0" :answer/id #uuid "0b53377f-4bd5-5365-9c10-2ca3b11194d0" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/5c/5c309f8a49b3c3afe32a49d47f100f0f3ef9690541ced08d3d52c325f91ebf2a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-541ae966-8a6c-5dc2-a2a7-73778aa00ec1" :answer/id #uuid "541ae966-8a6c-5dc2-a2a7-73778aa00ec1" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/61/617c1457f99cf5e94f57d14de7723328cdf31622500e506a72cc873acdfcd99e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5c8c8e1-f272-595a-96c9-7d04213ad8aa" :answer/id #uuid "a5c8c8e1-f272-595a-96c9-7d04213ad8aa" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/86/86598866633d3a49c13cf979b0c2c9de74d147e4584a1c6a88847434bc9d3bbf.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7335149e-fb88-517d-9f8a-6c7ddaf63411" :answer/id #uuid "7335149e-fb88-517d-9f8a-6c7ddaf63411" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b1/b1be6f09b7f25c335ca10e89ff3b896652b27691ec6e5faca68e2872f219e835.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198779 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=N\\times N\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in N,\\,y\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/34/34f779801b1bb52d5834ab4e04c91d92585af9876f5ea687ad59cb6931e0144c.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}=\\{(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/38/387709ae58ce7716338785f82e9159ce8570b046518554edd820c3ed31a23c53.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $N\\times N$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/14/141d0b84041957cf8415db4673c999f5213ff23553e57ab7e0e9120e648f306c.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287777 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times Z\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in Z\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/96/9673951b4e72ba503e740369462f9e528e43ffc083706ee558ade7ccd52702f5.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}=\\{(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/79/797b66a293eb241f841869abe97e324f4301907f71029acceefd68487bef1b4f.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup \\text{ }{S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times Z$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/de/de19fcf0f246b09d4bb957568e026b36de3f55c1a6c7065d79faacee794a8326.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287784 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a negative integer and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative integer.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/37/37ad5627c3fb7cc89e751bb697e01ac7fecaf2712e230b6e3812ad9137d5cd46.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{-2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-2}=\\{(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/97/97ebcac85477b8e88564ab67d59c926d5faa72138c8a31d80d3b89895f33668c.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…{S}_{-5},{S}_{-4},{S}_{-3}$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…{S}_{-5}\\cup {S}_{-4}\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1} \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in {Z}^{-}}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize ${Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a7/a756e53554cb9184d96a52b83038fbd6b00b21d0a12cc658aebb5010e917fd03.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186284751 :question/problem "Let $I=[-1,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [-|i|,|i|].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=-0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{-0.5}=\\{-0.5\\}\\times [-0.5,0.5]=\\{(-0.5,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(-0.5,-0.5)$ to the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b7/b7793d85e02ccd05c55c34971c90be087d58274eabd21f46fe09f08ccd60797b.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [-|i|,|i|]=\\{(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-|i|,|i|]\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,-|i|)$ to the point $(i,|i|).$ \n\n![](images/c3/c30712295adfe51c655662cc09f8f1e23b3ea97573693154e0e0b6ba48e15c07.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/8c/8ce0c60aafe1a41b372c1f1477241c584d6f8ae224113c360576feb37fc25a8c.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186389923 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS=[-1,0]\\times [-1,1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,0]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in [-1,0],\\,y\\in [-1,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}=\\{(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,1]\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/c9/c93f3ad399b88ccbaac990f8f73e93cfdcb1192f52b9b12f2d3ce178776fa13b.png)\n\nNow, consider a general ${S}_{i}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some $i\\in [-1,0]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{i}=\\{(i,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,1]\\}\n$$\n\nFor each $i\\in [-1,0],$ ${S}_{i}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $(i,-1)$ to the point $(i,1).$\n\n![](images/65/6557201d202a401a92ee37c9a69eab9ab6ab87c6342dbb9d2afebae30141346f.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $I=[-1,0]:$\n\n$$\nS={⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}.\n$$\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following rectangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/97/97a74b119ddb8a618d6564bc077d03a5a4ceb7152702beb3682f2eab1a436b70.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287874 :question/problem "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [-i,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=\\{1\\}\\times [-1,1]=\\{(1,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-1,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(1,-1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f2/f26fa8a525806370f5505ad097ead2b289192bb3a6eb3e341eb7c2f91a03531f.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [-i,i]=\\{(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-i,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,-i)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](images/bf/bfeafe4c3b800b33b4f8550de02c2eb908effb5c28ef59b40f8c5e2bcad38ac4.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/4d/4d860b60ab9e139b26c9f6e1d330188d5e752a51624a2374ad713b6974e66608.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287873 :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times \\{1-i\\}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-0.5,0.5]\\times \\{0.5\\}=\\{(k,0.5)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ to the point $(0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/509d3e8d3bce0b422cad7e7f02377468cacc0de3a4e45647c12fef15f2c9991f.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times \\{1-i\\}=\\{(k,1-i)\\,:\\,k\\in [-i,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(-i,1-i)$ to the point $(i,1-i).$ \n\n![](images/21/21859c2ac02ba80d47672bf2fab25f7358feb921bc5b546c1e8c783c2819bea3.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/5b/5b5715ba2a1b71a9f5aef52882ed62ba2fe1610c143224914430f79fdc8f06b5.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287877 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,1]\\times [0,0.5]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,1],b\\in [0,0.5]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(0,1),$ $(0.5,1),$ and $(0.5,0)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/0f/0fef44c4b8c4911a892d7dad4502edbb80ef236553d6fddb57de1472bd44a62b.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,1]\\times [0,i]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,1],b\\in [0,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(0,1),$ $(i,1),$ and $(i,0).$\n\n![](images/f4/f4602a46d3e4fc1799a475f05548ef278fdd33d354d1ec19ca4e5c5cc34cfcf8.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segments $[0,1]\\times \\{0\\}:$\n\n![](images/19/19a2cd951a55508269a0034aec582345bf7e350ec516fe1eecac0d6bf5847981.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186284731 :question/problem "Let $I=[1,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,2-i]\\times \\{i\\}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=[0,2-1]\\times \\{1\\}=[0,1]\\times \\{1\\}=\\{(k,1)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/ddb7039dfcdbc2ef4070ce732c635a48194b6675cc3c78de6267e656c5e8387a.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,2-i]\\times \\{i\\}=\\{(k,i)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,2-i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(0,i)$ to the point $(2-i,i).$ \n\n![](images/e8/e83644a2f0de96ee58ea893028bb7a42681ee71a15d194f2b344c4c15f60fafe.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186284732 :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=\\frac{1}{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1/2}=\\{\\frac{1}{2}\\}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]=\\{\\frac{1}{2}\\}\\times [0,1]=\\{(\\frac{1}{2},k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0)$ to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/28/2827c5b33e84aff775d8bcc7ca98492afb220dda3a52fec4166afaa4da187d66.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)]=\\{(i,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi k))\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)).$ \n\n![](images/5a/5a108a3c0cdf37a072d135674b6ffdb3894b8c5ba142604ea7a434b0f42245f5.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186284750 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-1,1]\\times [-0.5,0.5]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-0.5,0.5]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-0.5),$ $(1,0.5),$ $(-1,0.5),$ and $(-1,-0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f0a530434b8ac38fdb4cb517f22e121892c8b7c635c702c1cd9fccb2961aee18.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-1,1]\\times [-i,i]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-i,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-i),$ $(1,i),$ $(-1,i),$ and $(-1,-i).$\n\n![](images/69/6982658bd8ce0e3c81953309ed9f2a70d1e56bbfce8af8862a32da66bf19e638.png)\n\n \n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $[-1,1]\\times \\{0\\}:$\n\n![](images/34/34a3addbdcf3d5348805f494ed2e427f21f6ad424b0d9d2a411dae9f99b2fb7a.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}] "assertions" [[17592186287870 :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=\\{-2,2\\}\\times \\{-1,0,1\\}$ in the Cartesian plane?"] [17592186287870 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing $\\{-2,2\\}\\times \\{-1,0,1\\}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS=\\{(-2,-1),(-2,0),(-2,1),(2,-1),(2,0),(2,1)\\}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/5d/5dd3ff71d17188adaa68632b6d16aed1f41e1975ec4ddd321a8160fabb0e16b8.png)"] ["question-871c3eee-78f1-5d74-b0c7-258b8f08a0ed" :question/id #uuid "871c3eee-78f1-5d74-b0c7-258b8f08a0ed"] ["question-871c3eee-78f1-5d74-b0c7-258b8f08a0ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-182004"] ["question-871c3eee-78f1-5d74-b0c7-258b8f08a0ed" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=\\{-2,1,2\\}\\times [0,2]$ in the Cartesian plane?"] ["question-871c3eee-78f1-5d74-b0c7-258b8f08a0ed" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=\\{-2,1,2\\}\\times [0,2]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $-2,1,$ or $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,2].$\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in \\{-2,1,2\\},\\,y\\in [0,2]\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,2].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,2]\\}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/44/449b948df4a32c540281ddac1659c261e2908433ecc1f383b26cb5cb4aa29756.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,2].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}=\\{(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,2]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/92/922c96e62e8ed3481e595595e060956fbaf191cd3ec7636e7a874b11fea3bcd5.png)\n\nNow, consider ${S}_{3}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,2].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}=\\{(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,2]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/0d/0d3cb62f7e9fb47823d0a2ba8c071e09adbea673ad54a5d1b014e8882aeff472.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the three line segments shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7cecda03e84263537cb29bbd14bb4dccc2e60fada10db1ca96798490e978676.png)"] ["question-871c3eee-78f1-5d74-b0c7-258b8f08a0ed" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-120de378-6c5c-5a20-a5a4-fe230e702451" :answer-field/id #uuid "120de378-6c5c-5a20-a5a4-fe230e702451"] ["answer-c442a1fb-c5aa-593e-8502-90bebc8b66ae" :answer/id #uuid "c442a1fb-c5aa-593e-8502-90bebc8b66ae"] ["answer-c442a1fb-c5aa-593e-8502-90bebc8b66ae" :answer/type :answer.type/image] ["answer-c442a1fb-c5aa-593e-8502-90bebc8b66ae" :answer/value "images/13/13a0024b41d50d0ad22f790246e17ec19ce1e9bf91c7e2fe734bfcbdb9a00ef9.png"] ["answer-1cb0b49a-e727-5e96-8f08-b6642b88f5fb" :answer/id #uuid "1cb0b49a-e727-5e96-8f08-b6642b88f5fb"] ["answer-1cb0b49a-e727-5e96-8f08-b6642b88f5fb" :answer/type :answer.type/image] ["answer-1cb0b49a-e727-5e96-8f08-b6642b88f5fb" :answer/value "images/21/21119e9bd9b5a183cbf686891ba1b1fb33f645fbff27f1e088af5a88e5eea026.png"] ["answer-3f98f313-1f05-5432-9f13-6963c25952a4" :answer/id #uuid "3f98f313-1f05-5432-9f13-6963c25952a4"] ["answer-3f98f313-1f05-5432-9f13-6963c25952a4" :answer/type :answer.type/image] ["answer-3f98f313-1f05-5432-9f13-6963c25952a4" :answer/value "images/a4/a4b3570ea09a38f044c6926f0516ddf9c42f9af6953422e3f2b90bca07bf70e9.png"] ["answer-d7da440a-e81b-550c-ac55-bea9848fa4dc" :answer/id #uuid "d7da440a-e81b-550c-ac55-bea9848fa4dc"] ["answer-d7da440a-e81b-550c-ac55-bea9848fa4dc" :answer/type :answer.type/image] ["answer-d7da440a-e81b-550c-ac55-bea9848fa4dc" :answer/value "images/b8/b871bd5b8829c981f43bfbf8c051d50ee7a389df6c706f5d48fd07e167d9a66e.png"] ["answer-8ef1cf93-ea8f-5c4a-bd63-007805fb0442" :answer/id #uuid "8ef1cf93-ea8f-5c4a-bd63-007805fb0442"] ["answer-8ef1cf93-ea8f-5c4a-bd63-007805fb0442" :answer/type :answer.type/image] ["answer-8ef1cf93-ea8f-5c4a-bd63-007805fb0442" :answer/value "images/f7/f7cecda03e84263537cb29bbd14bb4dccc2e60fada10db1ca96798490e978676.png"] ["answer-field-120de378-6c5c-5a20-a5a4-fe230e702451" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-120de378-6c5c-5a20-a5a4-fe230e702451" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-120de378-6c5c-5a20-a5a4-fe230e702451" :answer-field/correct "answer-8ef1cf93-ea8f-5c4a-bd63-007805fb0442"] ["answer-field-120de378-6c5c-5a20-a5a4-fe230e702451" :answer-field/choices "answer-c442a1fb-c5aa-593e-8502-90bebc8b66ae"] ["answer-field-120de378-6c5c-5a20-a5a4-fe230e702451" :answer-field/choices "answer-1cb0b49a-e727-5e96-8f08-b6642b88f5fb"] ["answer-field-120de378-6c5c-5a20-a5a4-fe230e702451" :answer-field/choices "answer-3f98f313-1f05-5432-9f13-6963c25952a4"] ["answer-field-120de378-6c5c-5a20-a5a4-fe230e702451" :answer-field/choices "answer-d7da440a-e81b-550c-ac55-bea9848fa4dc"] ["answer-field-120de378-6c5c-5a20-a5a4-fe230e702451" :answer-field/choices "answer-8ef1cf93-ea8f-5c4a-bd63-007805fb0442"] ["question-871c3eee-78f1-5d74-b0c7-258b8f08a0ed" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-120de378-6c5c-5a20-a5a4-fe230e702451"] [17592186198272 :knowledge-point/questions "question-871c3eee-78f1-5d74-b0c7-258b8f08a0ed"] [17592186198802 :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=\\{0,1,2\\}\\times \\{-1,1\\}$ in the Cartesian plane?"] [17592186198802 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing $\\{0,1,2\\}\\times \\{-1,1\\}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS=\\{(0,-1),(0,1),(1,-1),(1,1),(2,-1),(2,1)\\}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/c5/c5fdae9b803df3873ee7deef4a36678e0d8274dd256fe331360261f1fe6dd492.png)"] ["question-68fa150f-fc38-56f5-947b-bb3d8c8fe527" :question/id #uuid "68fa150f-fc38-56f5-947b-bb3d8c8fe527"] ["question-68fa150f-fc38-56f5-947b-bb3d8c8fe527" :question/math-academy-id "q-184490"] ["question-68fa150f-fc38-56f5-947b-bb3d8c8fe527" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=\\{-2,0,2\\}\\times \\{1,4\\}$ in the Cartesian plane?"] ["question-68fa150f-fc38-56f5-947b-bb3d8c8fe527" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing $\\{-2,0,2\\}\\times \\{1,4\\}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS=\\{(-2,1),(-2,4),(0,1),(0,4),(2,1),(2,4)\\}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/86/86598866633d3a49c13cf979b0c2c9de74d147e4584a1c6a88847434bc9d3bbf.png)"] ["question-68fa150f-fc38-56f5-947b-bb3d8c8fe527" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-9f0af83d-008f-57e3-8335-76248a77c51e" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f0af83d-008f-57e3-8335-76248a77c51e"] ["answer-06dab824-32e7-5fbe-a9fd-1e57e69a4a62" :answer/id #uuid "06dab824-32e7-5fbe-a9fd-1e57e69a4a62"] ["answer-06dab824-32e7-5fbe-a9fd-1e57e69a4a62" :answer/type :answer.type/image] ["answer-06dab824-32e7-5fbe-a9fd-1e57e69a4a62" :answer/value "images/05/05721c578d17e26442cb64b114e64d52fb3822e2cd33a852144be05669087737.png"] ["answer-0b53377f-4bd5-5365-9c10-2ca3b11194d0" :answer/id #uuid "0b53377f-4bd5-5365-9c10-2ca3b11194d0"] ["answer-0b53377f-4bd5-5365-9c10-2ca3b11194d0" :answer/type :answer.type/image] ["answer-0b53377f-4bd5-5365-9c10-2ca3b11194d0" :answer/value "images/5c/5c309f8a49b3c3afe32a49d47f100f0f3ef9690541ced08d3d52c325f91ebf2a.png"] ["answer-541ae966-8a6c-5dc2-a2a7-73778aa00ec1" :answer/id #uuid "541ae966-8a6c-5dc2-a2a7-73778aa00ec1"] ["answer-541ae966-8a6c-5dc2-a2a7-73778aa00ec1" :answer/type :answer.type/image] ["answer-541ae966-8a6c-5dc2-a2a7-73778aa00ec1" :answer/value "images/61/617c1457f99cf5e94f57d14de7723328cdf31622500e506a72cc873acdfcd99e.png"] ["answer-a5c8c8e1-f272-595a-96c9-7d04213ad8aa" :answer/id #uuid "a5c8c8e1-f272-595a-96c9-7d04213ad8aa"] ["answer-a5c8c8e1-f272-595a-96c9-7d04213ad8aa" :answer/type :answer.type/image] ["answer-a5c8c8e1-f272-595a-96c9-7d04213ad8aa" :answer/value "images/86/86598866633d3a49c13cf979b0c2c9de74d147e4584a1c6a88847434bc9d3bbf.png"] ["answer-7335149e-fb88-517d-9f8a-6c7ddaf63411" :answer/id #uuid "7335149e-fb88-517d-9f8a-6c7ddaf63411"] ["answer-7335149e-fb88-517d-9f8a-6c7ddaf63411" :answer/type :answer.type/image] ["answer-7335149e-fb88-517d-9f8a-6c7ddaf63411" :answer/value "images/b1/b1be6f09b7f25c335ca10e89ff3b896652b27691ec6e5faca68e2872f219e835.png"] ["answer-field-9f0af83d-008f-57e3-8335-76248a77c51e" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-9f0af83d-008f-57e3-8335-76248a77c51e" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-9f0af83d-008f-57e3-8335-76248a77c51e" :answer-field/correct "answer-a5c8c8e1-f272-595a-96c9-7d04213ad8aa"] ["answer-field-9f0af83d-008f-57e3-8335-76248a77c51e" :answer-field/choices "answer-06dab824-32e7-5fbe-a9fd-1e57e69a4a62"] ["answer-field-9f0af83d-008f-57e3-8335-76248a77c51e" :answer-field/choices "answer-0b53377f-4bd5-5365-9c10-2ca3b11194d0"] ["answer-field-9f0af83d-008f-57e3-8335-76248a77c51e" :answer-field/choices "answer-541ae966-8a6c-5dc2-a2a7-73778aa00ec1"] ["answer-field-9f0af83d-008f-57e3-8335-76248a77c51e" :answer-field/choices "answer-a5c8c8e1-f272-595a-96c9-7d04213ad8aa"] ["answer-field-9f0af83d-008f-57e3-8335-76248a77c51e" :answer-field/choices "answer-7335149e-fb88-517d-9f8a-6c7ddaf63411"] ["question-68fa150f-fc38-56f5-947b-bb3d8c8fe527" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-9f0af83d-008f-57e3-8335-76248a77c51e"] [17592186198272 :knowledge-point/questions "question-68fa150f-fc38-56f5-947b-bb3d8c8fe527"] [17592186198779 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=N\\times N\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in N,\\,y\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/34/34f779801b1bb52d5834ab4e04c91d92585af9876f5ea687ad59cb6931e0144c.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}=\\{(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/38/387709ae58ce7716338785f82e9159ce8570b046518554edd820c3ed31a23c53.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $N\\times N$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/14/141d0b84041957cf8415db4673c999f5213ff23553e57ab7e0e9120e648f306c.png)"] [17592186287777 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times Z\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in Z\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/96/9673951b4e72ba503e740369462f9e528e43ffc083706ee558ade7ccd52702f5.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}=\\{(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/79/797b66a293eb241f841869abe97e324f4301907f71029acceefd68487bef1b4f.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup \\text{ }{S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times Z$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/de/de19fcf0f246b09d4bb957568e026b36de3f55c1a6c7065d79faacee794a8326.png)"] [17592186287784 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a negative integer and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative integer.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/37/37ad5627c3fb7cc89e751bb697e01ac7fecaf2712e230b6e3812ad9137d5cd46.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{-2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-2}=\\{(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/97/97ebcac85477b8e88564ab67d59c926d5faa72138c8a31d80d3b89895f33668c.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…{S}_{-5},{S}_{-4},{S}_{-3}$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…{S}_{-5}\\cup {S}_{-4}\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1} \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in {Z}^{-}}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize ${Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a7/a756e53554cb9184d96a52b83038fbd6b00b21d0a12cc658aebb5010e917fd03.png)"] [17592186284751 :question/problem "Let $I=[-1,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [-|i|,|i|].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$"] [17592186284751 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=-0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{-0.5}=\\{-0.5\\}\\times [-0.5,0.5]=\\{(-0.5,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(-0.5,-0.5)$ to the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b7/b7793d85e02ccd05c55c34971c90be087d58274eabd21f46fe09f08ccd60797b.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [-|i|,|i|]=\\{(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-|i|,|i|]\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,-|i|)$ to the point $(i,|i|).$ \n\n![](images/c3/c30712295adfe51c655662cc09f8f1e23b3ea97573693154e0e0b6ba48e15c07.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/8c/8ce0c60aafe1a41b372c1f1477241c584d6f8ae224113c360576feb37fc25a8c.png)"] [17592186389923 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS=[-1,0]\\times [-1,1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,0]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in [-1,0],\\,y\\in [-1,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}=\\{(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,1]\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/c9/c93f3ad399b88ccbaac990f8f73e93cfdcb1192f52b9b12f2d3ce178776fa13b.png)\n\nNow, consider a general ${S}_{i}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some $i\\in [-1,0]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{i}=\\{(i,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,1]\\}\n$$\n\nFor each $i\\in [-1,0],$ ${S}_{i}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $(i,-1)$ to the point $(i,1).$\n\n![](images/65/6557201d202a401a92ee37c9a69eab9ab6ab87c6342dbb9d2afebae30141346f.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $I=[-1,0]:$\n\n$$\nS={⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}.\n$$\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following rectangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/97/97a74b119ddb8a618d6564bc077d03a5a4ceb7152702beb3682f2eab1a436b70.png)"] [17592186287874 :question/problem "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [-i,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$"] [17592186287874 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=\\{1\\}\\times [-1,1]=\\{(1,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-1,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(1,-1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f2/f26fa8a525806370f5505ad097ead2b289192bb3a6eb3e341eb7c2f91a03531f.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [-i,i]=\\{(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-i,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,-i)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](images/bf/bfeafe4c3b800b33b4f8550de02c2eb908effb5c28ef59b40f8c5e2bcad38ac4.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/4d/4d860b60ab9e139b26c9f6e1d330188d5e752a51624a2374ad713b6974e66608.png)"] [17592186287873 :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times \\{1-i\\}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$"] [17592186287873 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-0.5,0.5]\\times \\{0.5\\}=\\{(k,0.5)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ to the point $(0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/509d3e8d3bce0b422cad7e7f02377468cacc0de3a4e45647c12fef15f2c9991f.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times \\{1-i\\}=\\{(k,1-i)\\,:\\,k\\in [-i,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(-i,1-i)$ to the point $(i,1-i).$ \n\n![](images/21/21859c2ac02ba80d47672bf2fab25f7358feb921bc5b546c1e8c783c2819bea3.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/5b/5b5715ba2a1b71a9f5aef52882ed62ba2fe1610c143224914430f79fdc8f06b5.png)"] [17592186287877 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,1]\\times [0,0.5]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,1],b\\in [0,0.5]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(0,1),$ $(0.5,1),$ and $(0.5,0)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/0f/0fef44c4b8c4911a892d7dad4502edbb80ef236553d6fddb57de1472bd44a62b.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,1]\\times [0,i]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,1],b\\in [0,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(0,1),$ $(i,1),$ and $(i,0).$\n\n![](images/f4/f4602a46d3e4fc1799a475f05548ef278fdd33d354d1ec19ca4e5c5cc34cfcf8.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segments $[0,1]\\times \\{0\\}:$\n\n![](images/19/19a2cd951a55508269a0034aec582345bf7e350ec516fe1eecac0d6bf5847981.png)"] [17592186284731 :question/problem "Let $I=[1,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,2-i]\\times \\{i\\}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$"] [17592186284731 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=[0,2-1]\\times \\{1\\}=[0,1]\\times \\{1\\}=\\{(k,1)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/ddb7039dfcdbc2ef4070ce732c635a48194b6675cc3c78de6267e656c5e8387a.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,2-i]\\times \\{i\\}=\\{(k,i)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,2-i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(0,i)$ to the point $(2-i,i).$ \n\n![](images/e8/e83644a2f0de96ee58ea893028bb7a42681ee71a15d194f2b344c4c15f60fafe.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set."] [17592186284732 :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$"] [17592186284732 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=\\frac{1}{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1/2}=\\{\\frac{1}{2}\\}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]=\\{\\frac{1}{2}\\}\\times [0,1]=\\{(\\frac{1}{2},k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0)$ to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/28/2827c5b33e84aff775d8bcc7ca98492afb220dda3a52fec4166afaa4da187d66.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)]=\\{(i,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi k))\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)).$ \n\n![](images/5a/5a108a3c0cdf37a072d135674b6ffdb3894b8c5ba142604ea7a434b0f42245f5.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set."] [17592186284750 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-1,1]\\times [-0.5,0.5]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-0.5,0.5]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-0.5),$ $(1,0.5),$ $(-1,0.5),$ and $(-1,-0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f0a530434b8ac38fdb4cb517f22e121892c8b7c635c702c1cd9fccb2961aee18.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-1,1]\\times [-i,i]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-i,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-i),$ $(1,i),$ $(-1,i),$ and $(-1,-i).$\n\n![](images/69/6982658bd8ce0e3c81953309ed9f2a70d1e56bbfce8af8862a32da66bf19e638.png)\n\n \n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $[-1,1]\\times \\{0\\}:$\n\n![](images/34/34a3addbdcf3d5348805f494ed2e427f21f6ad424b0d9d2a411dae9f99b2fb7a.png)"]] "retractions" [[17592186287870 :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={-2,2}\\times {-1,0,1}$ in the Cartesian plane?"] [17592186287870 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing ${-2,2}\\times {-1,0,1}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS={(-2,-1),(-2,0),(-2,1),(2,-1),(2,0),(2,1)}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/5d/5dd3ff71d17188adaa68632b6d16aed1f41e1975ec4ddd321a8160fabb0e16b8.png)"] [17592186198802 :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={0,1,2}\\times {-1,1}$ in the Cartesian plane?"] [17592186198802 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing ${0,1,2}\\times {-1,1}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS={(0,-1),(0,1),(1,-1),(1,1),(2,-1),(2,1)}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/c5/c5fdae9b803df3873ee7deef4a36678e0d8274dd256fe331360261f1fe6dd492.png)"] [17592186198779 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=N\\times N\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in N,\\,y\\in N}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/34/34f779801b1bb52d5834ab4e04c91d92585af9876f5ea687ad59cb6931e0144c.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/38/387709ae58ce7716338785f82e9159ce8570b046518554edd820c3ed31a23c53.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $N\\times N$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/14/141d0b84041957cf8415db4673c999f5213ff23553e57ab7e0e9120e648f306c.png)"] [17592186287777 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times Z\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/96/9673951b4e72ba503e740369462f9e528e43ffc083706ee558ade7ccd52702f5.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}={(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/79/797b66a293eb241f841869abe97e324f4301907f71029acceefd68487bef1b4f.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup \\text{ }{S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times Z$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/de/de19fcf0f246b09d4bb957568e026b36de3f55c1a6c7065d79faacee794a8326.png)"] [17592186287784 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a negative integer and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative integer.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/37/37ad5627c3fb7cc89e751bb697e01ac7fecaf2712e230b6e3812ad9137d5cd46.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{-2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-2}={(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/97/97ebcac85477b8e88564ab67d59c926d5faa72138c8a31d80d3b89895f33668c.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…{S}_{-5},{S}_{-4},{S}_{-3}$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…{S}_{-5}\\cup {S}_{-4}\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1} \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in {Z}^{-}}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize ${Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a7/a756e53554cb9184d96a52b83038fbd6b00b21d0a12cc658aebb5010e917fd03.png)"] [17592186284751 :question/problem "Let $I=[-1,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [-|i|,|i|].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$"] [17592186284751 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=-0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{-0.5}={-0.5}\\times [-0.5,0.5]={(-0.5,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(-0.5,-0.5)$ to the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b7/b7793d85e02ccd05c55c34971c90be087d58274eabd21f46fe09f08ccd60797b.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [-|i|,|i|]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-|i|,|i|]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,-|i|)$ to the point $(i,|i|).$ \n\n![](images/c3/c30712295adfe51c655662cc09f8f1e23b3ea97573693154e0e0b6ba48e15c07.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/8c/8ce0c60aafe1a41b372c1f1477241c584d6f8ae224113c360576feb37fc25a8c.png)"] [17592186389923 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS=[-1,0]\\times [-1,1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,0]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in [-1,0],\\,y\\in [-1,1]}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}={(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,1]}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/c9/c93f3ad399b88ccbaac990f8f73e93cfdcb1192f52b9b12f2d3ce178776fa13b.png)\n\nNow, consider a general ${S}_{i}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some $i\\in [-1,0]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{i}={(i,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,1]}\n$$\n\nFor each $i\\in [-1,0],$ ${S}_{i}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $(i,-1)$ to the point $(i,1).$\n\n![](images/65/6557201d202a401a92ee37c9a69eab9ab6ab87c6342dbb9d2afebae30141346f.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $I=[-1,0]:$\n\n$$\nS={⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}.\n$$\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following rectangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/97/97a74b119ddb8a618d6564bc077d03a5a4ceb7152702beb3682f2eab1a436b70.png)"] [17592186287874 :question/problem "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [-i,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$"] [17592186287874 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}={1}\\times [-1,1]={(1,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-1,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(1,-1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f2/f26fa8a525806370f5505ad097ead2b289192bb3a6eb3e341eb7c2f91a03531f.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [-i,i]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-i,i]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,-i)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](images/bf/bfeafe4c3b800b33b4f8550de02c2eb908effb5c28ef59b40f8c5e2bcad38ac4.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/4d/4d860b60ab9e139b26c9f6e1d330188d5e752a51624a2374ad713b6974e66608.png)"] [17592186287873 :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times {1-i}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$"] [17592186287873 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-0.5,0.5]\\times {0.5}={(k,0.5)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ to the point $(0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/509d3e8d3bce0b422cad7e7f02377468cacc0de3a4e45647c12fef15f2c9991f.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times {1-i}={(k,1-i)\\,:\\,k\\in [-i,i]}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(-i,1-i)$ to the point $(i,1-i).$ \n\n![](images/21/21859c2ac02ba80d47672bf2fab25f7358feb921bc5b546c1e8c783c2819bea3.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/5b/5b5715ba2a1b71a9f5aef52882ed62ba2fe1610c143224914430f79fdc8f06b5.png)"] [17592186287877 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,1]\\times [0,0.5]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,1],b\\in [0,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(0,1),$ $(0.5,1),$ and $(0.5,0)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/0f/0fef44c4b8c4911a892d7dad4502edbb80ef236553d6fddb57de1472bd44a62b.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,1]\\times [0,i]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,1],b\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(0,1),$ $(i,1),$ and $(i,0).$\n\n![](images/f4/f4602a46d3e4fc1799a475f05548ef278fdd33d354d1ec19ca4e5c5cc34cfcf8.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segments $[0,1]\\times {0}:$\n\n![](images/19/19a2cd951a55508269a0034aec582345bf7e350ec516fe1eecac0d6bf5847981.png)"] [17592186284731 :question/problem "Let $I=[1,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,2-i]\\times {i}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$"] [17592186284731 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=[0,2-1]\\times {1}=[0,1]\\times {1}={(k,1)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/ddb7039dfcdbc2ef4070ce732c635a48194b6675cc3c78de6267e656c5e8387a.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,2-i]\\times {i}={(k,i)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,2-i]}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(0,i)$ to the point $(2-i,i).$ \n\n![](images/e8/e83644a2f0de96ee58ea893028bb7a42681ee71a15d194f2b344c4c15f60fafe.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set."] [17592186284732 :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$"] [17592186284732 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=\\frac{1}{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1/2}={\\frac{1}{2}}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]={\\frac{1}{2}}\\times [0,1]={(\\frac{1}{2},k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0)$ to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/28/2827c5b33e84aff775d8bcc7ca98492afb220dda3a52fec4166afaa4da187d66.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)]={(i,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi k))\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)).$ \n\n![](images/5a/5a108a3c0cdf37a072d135674b6ffdb3894b8c5ba142604ea7a434b0f42245f5.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set."] [17592186284750 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-1,1]\\times [-0.5,0.5]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-0.5),$ $(1,0.5),$ $(-1,0.5),$ and $(-1,-0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f0a530434b8ac38fdb4cb517f22e121892c8b7c635c702c1cd9fccb2961aee18.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-1,1]\\times [-i,i]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-i,i]}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-i),$ $(1,i),$ $(-1,i),$ and $(-1,-i).$\n\n![](images/69/6982658bd8ce0e3c81953309ed9f2a70d1e56bbfce8af8862a32da66bf19e638.png)\n\n \n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $[-1,1]\\times {0}:$\n\n![](images/34/34a3addbdcf3d5348805f494ed2e427f21f6ad424b0d9d2a411dae9f99b2fb7a.png)"]]}]